                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "A surprisingly difficult and fun Megaman-type game with anime girls as the main characters. The fun in this game lies in the multiple layers to the gameplay. There's a leveling system on the map, a leveling system in game and a leveling system that exists as part of the game's menu overlay after you complete it. The game is designed for repeat runs since the main focus is on the achievements. That said, the achievements are generally hard.\r\n\r\nYou start the game as \"Zizou\" and unlock a second character after beating the game. Each boss grants a weapon that makes another boss easier to kill (like in Megaman) but with sufficient skill you can also kill the bosses using your default gun. On normal and hard there are no health pickups. The stages are pretty simplistic and linear, with no instakills except falling into pits. The bosses are really the meat of the game play, and each difficulty amps up their attack patterns and switches things up in a subtle and eventually very cruel way.\r\n\r\nBeating the game allows you to play as \"Riva\" and she is much harder to use because her weapon has no range by default and she can't increase her actual attack power in combat. Defeating the final boss in Riva's game was by far the hardest challenge and harder than anything I've ever seen in a Megaman game. It took me a few hours just to figure out how to kill the \"boss gauntlet\" before the final boss effectivlely, and then the final boss was actually a nightmare because it's designed to throw you back just when it seems you're about to win.\r\n\r\nOne major issue with the game's design is that there's no pause between enemy attack pattern switches. This was probably deliberate but it doesn't make it any less frustrating. This means a boss may go from launching rings to suddenly throwing a nearly undodgeable wind attack without any notice. Still, the actual process of final beating the final bosses on lunatic is very satisfying because they are designed to make your heart race and your palms sweat as you eventually approach the end.\r\n\r\nThe other achievements are only easier because you can do them on the easiest difficulty, including beating the game in less than five hits and killing each boss without taking damage. There are hidden powerups in the stages called \"Pure Syega\" but you will never find them without a guide since they are invisible and you have to shoot or just accidentally run into them. If you collect all of them you can get a shield ability that makes the final section much easier. \r\n\r\nOverall I ended up doing about eight different runs to unlock all the achievements and I had a pretty satisfying experience with this. It's kind of like \"Touhou-style Megaman\" a game that possesses both a unique style and a surprising amount of substance due to all the different upgrade layers. It's probably one of the best Megaman clones I've played, but it's hard not to look past some of the issues. Most of the problems just stem from normal and hard being made deliberately broken. Riva can unlock a dash but dashing into things kills you. You can dodge by double tapping \"left\" or \"right\" but this is extremely difficult to pull off and even when you do pull it off you might immediately hit another projectile, negating the whole thing. Your character's hitbox is also really big and there are certain \"projectile dodge\" bits similar to flying shooters that are way more frustrating because usually in flying shooting games your hitbox is smaller to allow for near-hits. \r\n\r\nStill, the animation is absolutely beautiful and reaching those final nailbiting moments in the final fights is satisfying because I kept discovering new things to make each attempt easier. I had a ton of fun with this game and it's a gem. If the environments were a bit more complex and the hard difficulties a bit fairer this might be one of my favorite games ever made.",
